## From bytes to agents

You started with a guessing game: what word comes next? Everything since has been about how a
machine plays that game, and what gets built around it. Zoom in and out of the whole picture; each
part links back to the chapter that explains it.

<WholePicture />

In one paragraph: a language model gives a **probability** for every possible next token (<ChapterRef to="guessing-game" through="probability-and-sampling" />).
Text becomes **tokens** (<ChapterRef to="text-is-numbers" />, <ChapterRef to="tokens" bare />), each token becomes a **vector** (<ChapterRef to="embeddings" />), and a stack of
**transformer blocks** refines those vectors, with **attention** letting each token use earlier ones
(<ChapterRef to="the-context-problem" through="the-transformer-block" />). The numbers inside are learned by **training** that nudges millions or billions of
weights to make the right tokens more likely (<ChapterRef to="a-neuron" through="learning-from-mistakes" />, <ChapterRef to="scale" bare />). Generating text is a **loop** of
predicting and picking (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />). Those weights can be rounded to fewer bits to save memory (<ChapterRef to="quantization" />),
and a small model can draft tokens for a big one to check, so it writes the same text faster
(<ChapterRef to="speculative-decoding" />). Tuning turns the model into an **assistant** (<ChapterRef to="predictor-to-assistant" />), and reasoning
models are tuned to write their working first (<ChapterRef to="reasoning-models" />). Prompting is choosing the document the model
continues: the wording, examples, instructions, and layout all shift what comes next, often in ways
you have to test to find out (<ChapterRef to="prompts-as-documents" through="where-prompts-break" />). A **harness** wraps the model with prompts, tools, an agent
loop, context management, caching, and guardrails against prompt injection (<ChapterRef to="the-model-is-not-the-product" through="trust-and-guardrails" />). Other kinds
of models change one piece and keep the rest: encoders read both ways, mixtures of experts use part
of themselves per token, recurrent models keep a fixed memory, vision models read patches, and
diffusion models paint from noise (<ChapterRef to="encoders" through="diffusion" />).

<RecapCard>

- A language model predicts the next token, nothing more, and does it very well at scale.
- What looks like a chat, a memory, or an agent is a harness calling that model in loops.
- Most quirks, from made-up facts to miscounted letters, follow from those two facts.
- Other models swap one piece, like the attention mask, the MLP, or what counts as a token, and
  keep the rest of the recipe.
- A prompt is the start of a document: everything in it shifts the next token, so test it.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** those are the pieces. In what order do they run when you send a message?
